findings.body:
`RolloutsSettingMessage` has the value `"%@ will improve features..."` but its translator comment says `%1$@`. The `.strings` files use `%@` in the comment. The l10n README says the reference-string linter requires every placeable in the text to be referenced in the comment, so `%@` is not covered and the `firefox-ios-l10n-linter` job will likely fail on this PR. Translators would also get the wrong placeholder hint.

::diff{file="firefox-ios/Shared/Strings.swift" lines="6257-6261"}

::suggestion{file="firefox-ios/Shared/Strings.swift" lines="6261-6261"}
        comment: "A short description that explains that Mozilla may change features remotely. %@ is the app name (e.g. Firefox).")

The same comment in `en-GB` and `en-CA` `Settings.strings` already says `%@`, so the Swift comment is the odd one out.

`formatStudiesText` passes `strings.detailTextStudies` to `String(format:)`, but that key still holds the pre-v148 text ("%@ may install and run studies from time to time."). The new Studies description is `detailTextStudiesV2`, which this PR adds, but the formatter never uses it. The helper has no callers today, so nothing is broken yet, but it's named for the new toggle, and the follow-up split would show the old copy if someone wires it up as written. `formatRolloutsText` correctly uses its new key.

